.TH SYNCMOUNT 1 "2026-11-07" "syncmount 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syncmount \- server-authoritative, eventually consistent FUSE filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B client
.I URL MOUNTPOINT
.RI [ client-options ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B server
.I URL
.RI [ server-options ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B import
.B \-\-from
.I DIR
.RI [ import-options ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.I URL MOUNTPOINT
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.I URL
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B status
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-state
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-seq-gaps
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.br
.B syncmount
.RI [ options ]
.B debug-conntrace
.RI [ \-\-limit " N" ]
.RI [ \-\-mount-id " ID" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syncmount
mounts one namespace as a local directory.
The server owns the authoritative tree, assigns a total order, appends an
event log and streams that log to every client.
A client keeps a full SQLite replica. A write buffers in memory and returns;
a background commit every 100 ms, or flush, close, fsync, truncate, unlink or
rename, writes it to the replica and the durable outbox. Namespace operations
commit before returning.
The mountpoint is a view over the replica database; it is not a backing
directory.
.PP
The server and every client derive the same pre-shared key from one master
password with Argon2id and HKDF-SHA256.
The handshake is one round trip:
.B HELLO
(plain body carrying a sealed \fBAUTH\fP)
and
.B WELCOME
(plain body carrying a server proof and a sealed \fBACCEPT\fP).
There are no separate
.B AUTH
or
.B ACCEPT
frames; steady-state frames are sealed under a per-session key.
A wrong password fails at the sealed
.B AUTH
in
.B HELLO .
.PP
This is not end-to-end encryption.
The server holds the master password, decrypts every operation, applies it to
a plaintext SQLite database and stores plaintext chunks.
The key protects the link against an eavesdropper who does not know the
password; it does not protect the data from the server.
See
.I docs/threat-model.md
in the source distribution.
.PP
A client that loses its transport or is kicked unmounts and exits with status
10.
The replica and the outbox stay in the client database; the next start resends
pending and previously sent operations.
An epoch change clears the replica and replays the compacted event log from
zero; pending outbox rows survive.
.PP
The 16-byte namespace id is
.BR BLAKE3("sm/v2/namespace" || PSK)[..16] .
It is derived from the password, not from the URL.
The URL only chooses the connection target and the HTTP namespace prefix.
Every client and the server must use the same password; a different password
derives a different id and a different key.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B client \fIURL MOUNTPOINT\fR
Mount the namespace at
.I MOUNTPOINT
and run until the transport fails or a signal arrives.
.I MOUNTPOINT
must be an existing absolute directory.
The path of
.I URL
is the namespace prefix; it must match the server and every other client.
The scheme must be
.BR http .
An
.B https
URL is rejected at dial with
.BR TlsUnsupported ;
the client has no TLS stack.
The client opens or creates its SQLite replica, takes an exclusive
.B flock
on the database file, and mounts the view.
A second process on the same database fails at startup (exit 2).
.TP
.B server \fIURL\fR
Run the authoritative sequencer.
Only the path of
.I URL
becomes the namespace prefix; the host and port of
.I URL
are ignored.
The server opens or creates its SQLite database, derives the PSK from the
master password, and serves
.B {prefix}/rpc
and
.B {prefix}/events
over HTTP/1.1.
The listen address is chosen by
.B \-\-listen
or
.BR \-\-port .
The server requires the master password even though it never sends it: it
decrypts operations, plans them against the authoritative state and stores
plaintext file data.
.TP
.B import \fB\-\-from\fR \fIDIR\fR
Import an existing POSIX directory tree into a client database without
mounting it.
The tree is walked without following symbolic links.
Directories, regular files, symbolic links and hard links are preserved,
including mode, owner, group and modification time.
Regular files are split into 64 KiB BLAKE3-addressed chunks.
Inodes are deterministic:
.BR BLAKE3("sm/v2/import" || path)[..16] ,
so importing the same tree on several machines yields the same ids.
Re-running an import against the same database is a no-op.
Names that belonged to the old daemon
.RB ( .syncmount* ", " .audit.log )
are skipped, and special files are refused.
The database then behaves like one filled through FUSE: the first connection
to an empty server uploads it through the normal seed path.
.TP
.B status
Print one line per running client from its status document.
The client publishes the document under
.I /run/syncmount
(or
.BR SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR ).
.TP
.B debug-state
Print the full status document, which is the replay and lifecycle state a
running client publishes.
.TP
.B debug-seq-gaps
Scan the connection trace for missing sequence numbers and print the first gap,
the last gap and the total count.
.TP
.B debug-conntrace
Print the most recent frame events, oldest first.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " FILTER"
Set the tracing filter.
The value is a level name
.RB ( trace ", " debug ", " info ", " warn ", " error )
or a full EnvFilter directive.
Precedence: this option, then
.BR RUST_LOG ,
then
.BR SYNCMOUNT_LOG ,
then
.BR info .
An invalid directive is a startup error.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-format " FORMAT"
Render logs as
.B text
(default, alias
.BR pretty )
or
.BR json .
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Accepted for unit files and container entrypoints.
Both modes already run in the foreground; the option is a no-op.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the software version, git revision, build target, build profile,
protocol version, frame version and WCP version.
.SS Client
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Allow every local user to access the mount.
Requires
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf ;
without it the client refuses to start.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
as the client SQLite database.
Overrides
.BR SYNCMOUNT_DB .
Without either, the path is
.IR state_dir / namespace /client.db ,
where
.I state_dir
is
.B SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR
or the XDG data directory, and
.I namespace
is the URL path with
.B /
replaced by
.BR _ ,
or
.B root
for an empty prefix.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " PATH"
Read the master password from
.IR PATH .
The file must be a regular file owned by the current effective user with mode
0600 or stricter.
One trailing newline is stripped; an empty file is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-key
Refused.
A password in argv is visible in
.B ps
and shell history.
Use
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD
or
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE
instead, or pass
.BR \-\-key\-file .
With neither variable set and no
.B \-\-key\-file
option, the client prompts on the terminal.
.SS Server
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Bind
.IR ADDR
.RB ( host:port
or
.IR ip:port ).
Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1:8080 .
Port 0 asks the kernel for an ephemeral port; the chosen address is logged.
.B SYNCMOUNT_LISTEN
provides the same value when the option is absent.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Bind
.B 127.0.0.1:PORT.
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-listen .
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
as the server SQLite database.
Default:
.B SYNCMOUNT_SERVER_DB
if set, otherwise
.B syncmount-server.db
in the current directory.
The server database holds the authoritative object, name and chunk tables,
the event log and the replay sidecar.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " PATH"
Read the master password from
.IR PATH .
The same ownership and mode rules as the client apply.
.SS Import
.TP
.BI \-\-from " DIR"
Source directory tree.
The path itself must be a directory, not a symbolic link to one.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Destination client database.
Default:
.B SYNCMOUNT_DB
if set, otherwise
\fIstate_dir\fR/\fInamespace\fR/client.db,
where
.I state_dir
is
.B SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR
or the XDG data directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " NAME"
Namespace directory used for the default database path.
Default:
.BR root .
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Clear the existing tree, outbox and chunk table before importing.
Without it, an already imported tree is updated in place and a conflicting
entry is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-seed
Generate the deterministic seed outbox events after importing.
The client would generate the same events on its first connection to an empty
server; this option makes the upload inspectable offline.
.TP
.BI \-\-cluster " HEX"
Use an explicit 16-byte namespace id, written as 32 hex characters.
Without it the id is derived from the master password, exactly as
.B client
does.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " PATH"
Read the master password from
.IR PATH .
.SS Status and debug
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine-readable JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-mount\-id " ID"
Restrict output to one mount id.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Number of connection-trace entries to print.
Default: 100.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD
The master password.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE
A file containing the master password.
The file must be a regular file, owned by the current effective user and mode
0600 or stricter.
The environment variable wins over the file variable; a
.B \-\-key\-file
option wins over both.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_DB
Client database path used when
.B \-\-db
is absent.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR
Base for the default client database path.
Without it the client uses
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME/syncmount ,
then
.BR $HOME/.local/share/syncmount ,
then
.BR /var/lib/syncmount .
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_SERVER_DB
Server database path used when
.B \-\-db
is absent.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
Base directory for per-mount runtime state.
Default:
.BR /run/syncmount .
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_LISTEN
Listen address used when
.B \-\-listen
is absent.
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_LOG
Tracing filter used when
.B RUST_LOG
is unset.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Tracing filter used when
.B \-\-log\-level
is absent.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base for the default client database when
.B SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.B FUSERMOUNT_PATH
Explicit path to
.BR fusermount3 .
.TP
.B SYNCMOUNT_REQUIRE_FUSE
Test-only: a FUSE test that cannot run is a failure instead of a skip.
.SH FILES
Paths below use the default base;
.B SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
replaces
.BR /run/syncmount .
.TP
.B /run/syncmount/\fIMID\fR/lock
Single-instance lock, mode 0600.
.TP
.B /run/syncmount/\fIMID\fR/status.json
Atomically replaced status document, mode 0600.
.TP
.B /run/syncmount/\fIMID\fR.json
The documented status document, mode 0600.
.TP
.BR client.db
The client replica, outbox, chunks and FUSE inode table.
Created with mode 0600 in a directory set to 0700.
.TP
.BR server.db
The server's authoritative state and event log.
SQLite creates it under the process umask; set
.B UMask=0077
in the unit and keep it in a directory set to 0700.
.TP
.B /dev/fuse
The FUSE device; the client checks that it can open it before mounting.
.TP
.B /etc/fuse.conf
Must contain
.B user_allow_other
for
.BR \-\-allow\-other .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a clean unmount after a signal.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, including an unknown argument or the refused
.BR \-\-key .
A second instance on the same database is a startup failure (exit 2).
.TP
.B 2
Startup failure: bad URL, missing or unreadable password, unusable
mountpoint, bind failure, missing FUSE prerequisites, a handshake or dial
failure, or an unavailable status document.
.TP
.B 3
Reserved for a corrupt audit journal.
The v2 client keeps no audit journal and does not return this status.
.TP
.B 4
Authentication failure: the server rejected the sealed
.B AUTH
in
.B HELLO ,
or the
.B WELCOME
proof did not verify.
.TP
.B 10
The transport failed or the client was kicked; the mount has been released.
The durable outbox is kept and resent on the next start.
A unit that restarts the mount must treat this status as final; see
.BR SYSTEMD .
.SH SYSTEMD
The client exits with status 10 whenever it loses the cluster.
A unit that restarts on failure would restart it into the same failure, so
list 10 in
.BR RestartPreventExitStatus .
Status 4 is also non-retryable until the password is fixed; list it as well if
the unit should stop on a wrong password.
.PP
.nf
[Unit]
Description=syncmount private client

[Service]
Type=simple
User=user
UMask=0077
RuntimeDirectory=syncmount-private
Environment=SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR=/run/syncmount-private
Environment=SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR=/home/user/.local/state/private
Environment=SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD_FILE=/home/user/.config/syncmount/master.pass
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/syncmount client http://sync.example.com/private /home/user/private
Restart=on-failure
RestartPreventExitStatus=10 4

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
.fi
.PP
.I User
selects the account that owns the mount and reads the password file.
Set
.B SYNCMOUNT_STATE_DIR
explicitly: a system service does not inherit the user's environment, and the
client database lives below it.
.B SYNCMOUNT_RUN_DIR
must be writable by that user;
.B RuntimeDirectory
creates the directory and removes it when the unit stops.
The mountpoint must already exist and be accessible to the same user.
The password file must be owned by and readable by that user.
.PP
Statuses 1 and 2 are configuration errors and do not improve with a restart.
Status 3 is reserved.
A clean unmount after a signal exits 0 and is not restarted by
.BR Restart=on\-failure .
The client does not reconnect on its own: after the network returns, start the
unit again; the outbox is resent from the client database.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a server and mount it on one host:
.PP
.nf
export SYNCMOUNT_MASTER_PASSWORD='correct horse battery staple'
syncmount server http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync --listen 127.0.0.1:8080 \\
  --db /var/lib/syncmount/sync.db &
mkdir -p /mnt/share
syncmount client http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync /mnt/share \\
  --db /var/lib/syncmount/client.db
.fi
.PP
The same invocation with the implicit aliases:
.PP
.nf
syncmount http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync --listen 127.0.0.1:8080 &
syncmount http://127.0.0.1:8080/sync /mnt/share
.fi
.PP
Use a password file instead of an environment variable:
.PP
.nf
install -m 0600 /dev/null /etc/syncmount/master.pass
printf '%s\\n' 'correct horse battery staple' >/etc/syncmount/master.pass
syncmount server http://127.0.0.1:8081/private --listen 127.0.0.1:8081 \\
  --db /var/lib/syncmount/private/server.db \\
  --key-file /etc/syncmount/master.pass
.fi
.PP
Import an existing tree before the first client start:
.PP
.nf
syncmount import --from /srv/private --namespace private \\
  --db /var/lib/syncmount/client.db --seed
syncmount client http://sync.example.com/private /home/user/private \\
  --db /var/lib/syncmount/client.db
.fi
.PP
Inspect a running client:
.PP
.nf
syncmount status
syncmount status --json
syncmount debug-conntrace --limit 20
syncmount debug-seq-gaps --json
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Every mutation is local-first. A write is volatile until it is committed to
the client SQLite database and the durable outbox by the background writer or
by flush, close, fsync, truncate, unlink or rename; a crash loses only the
uncommitted buffer. Namespace operations commit before the FUSE callback
returns. Each namespace operation is one SQLite transaction; rename and unlink
first commit the related inode's buffered content in a separate transaction,
then change the names in one transaction. A size-changing setattr is one
SETATTR operation with one outbox row; the client commits the new size, chunk
list and metadata together, and the server applies them in one transaction. A
crash leaves an operation fully visible or completely absent. A crash,
disconnect or kick keeps the outbox, and the next session resends it.
There is no in-process reconnect and no offline mode.
.PP
The outbox has a soft resident-memory budget of 1 GiB and one million
operations.
Crossing it logs one warning; writes continue.
A lagging replay or a stalled network never turns a local write into
.BR ENOSPC ;
.B ENOSPC
means the local SQLite disk is full.
.PP
A row in state
.B sent
is not retried within the same session.
There is no acknowledgement timeout or in-session retransmit; the next session
resets
.B sent
to
.B pending
and resends it.
The server deduplicates by
.IR op_id ,
so a resend after a lost acknowledgement does not create a second event.
.PP
A server rejection marks the outbox row
.B failed
and records the errno.
The local SQLite effect is not rolled back.
A rejected namespace operation can leave the replica diverged until an epoch
change or a new import; writes to an existing path converge by
last-writer-wins.
.PP
The server has a per-session downlink budget of 32 MiB and 512 data frames and
kicks a consumer that cannot drain it with
.BR QueueOverflow .
A watermark that regresses or exceeds
.I current_seq
is kicked as
.B Regression
or
.BR Overclaim .
A kicked client unmounts and exits with status 10; the next start replays and
resends.
.PP
Graceful shutdown drains for at most 30 seconds, compacts the event log and
kicks every session with
.BR EpochMismatch .
Compaction advances the epoch, resets
.I seq
to zero, rebuilds a minimal replayable log for the live tree and leaves
.I replay_floor
at one.
A client that sees a different epoch clears its replica and replays from zero.
A crash skips compaction; the next start resumes
.I epoch
and
.I seq
from the database.
.PP
Only valid UTF-8 names are accepted, at most 255 bytes and free of
.B /
and NUL; otherwise the operation returns
.B EINVAL
or
.BR ENAMETOOLONG .
Paths are capped at 4096 bytes.
Extended attributes are not stored:
.B getxattr
returns
.BR ENODATA ,
.B setxattr
and
.B removexattr
return
.BR ENOTSUP .
POSIX ACLs are not supported.
.B fcntl
record locks return
.BR ENOSYS ;
.B flock
is local to one mount.
.PP
One wire write aggregate and one event payload are capped at 4 MiB.
The server refuses a file larger than 4 GiB.
A large write is split by the client; an application that needs larger files
must split them itself.
.PP
Reads use the local replica and can serve stale data during the convergence
window.
Reads are allowed during
.BR JOINING ,
so a reader can see a mixed tree while replay is filling it; writes return
.BR EROFS .
.PP
The server and every client store plaintext SQLite databases.
The client database is created 0600; set
.B UMask=0077
for the server database.
Keep both in a 0700 directory and use full-disk or volume encryption when the
host disk is not trusted.
The server can read and modify everything; this is not an end-to-end
encrypted system.
.PP
There is no perfect forward secrecy, no password rotation and no revocation.
A leaked password decrypts all recorded traffic and opens every database.
A new password derives a new namespace id and requires new databases.
.PP
There is one server process per namespace and no high availability.
The server can delay, drop, duplicate or reorder frames; clients detect their
own sequence gaps and epoch changes, but cannot prove a cross-client reorder.
.PP
The full semantic boundary is in
.I docs/known-limitations.md
in the source distribution; the adversary model is in
.IR docs/threat-model.md .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse (8),
.BR fuse (4),
.BR systemd.service (5)
